//! volume — the one way into the file systems on a disk: kernel/tree,
//! kernel/fstab, kernel/files and kernel/procfs ask here for everything a
//! mounted volume holds, and only this module calls kernel/ext2 and
//! kernel/journal (tests/mpx6-ext2's SEAM-CALLERS holds that).
//!
//! Today there is one on-disk format behind it, ext2 and its journalled
//! form ext3, so each item below is ext2's or the journal's own, under the
//! same name, with the same arguments and answer; the journal's four take
//! a `journal_` prefix, since `size` is a volume's file's size here. The
//! constants are ext2's values, spelled again (wolf cannot yet give a
//! module constant another module's; tests/mpx6-ext2's SEAM-CONSTS keeps
//! them equal). Nothing is kept here: no state, no lock, no call
//! between a caller and kernel/ext2, so kernel/block's buffer rule
//! (an address good until the next `get`) and kernel/ext2's write order
//! hold through it unchanged. A second format sits beside ext2 here, behind
//! the same names, when one is added.
//!
//! One CPU, IF clear for the whole of every call (as kernel/ext2).

use ext2
use journal

/// kernel/ext2's `LINK_MAX`, as it is there.
pub const LINK_MAX: u64 = 65000

/// kernel/ext2's `ROOT_INO`, as it is there.
pub const ROOT_INO: u64 = 2

/// kernel/ext2's `T_ATIME`, as it is there.
pub const T_ATIME: u64 = 1

/// kernel/ext2's `T_CTIME`, as it is there.
pub const T_CTIME: u64 = 4

/// kernel/ext2's `T_MTIME`, as it is there.
pub const T_MTIME: u64 = 2

/// kernel/ext2's `VOLS`, as it is there.
pub const VOLS: u64 = 4

